  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nRest of Today-Tuesday...Satellite imagery shows TD 2 spinning over\nthe NE Gulf this afternoon. Bands of showers with embedded\nlightning storms on the outer fringes of the area of moisture\nassociated with this system have developed across east central\nFlorida. This activity has been quick-moving at around 25-35 mph.\nThus, it hasn't taken much to produce wind gusts near 40 mph,\naided by steep low-level lapse rates. Expect scattered showers and\nstorms to persist into this evening, with a collision with the\neast coast sea breeze possible near the Volusia County coast. Main\nthreats will continue to be wind gusts up to 55 mph and lightning\nstrikes, though can't rule out a quick spin up along any boundary\ncollisions. Convection looks to diminish with the loss of daytime\nheating. However, a few showers and storms will remain possible\nacross the Atlantic waters overnight and may brush the coast at\ntimes. Lows tonight remain warm, in the mid to upper 70s.\n\nTD 2 (perhaps as a TS) looks to linger over the NE Gulf Tuesday.\nHowever, will see some drift to the west begin, as surface ridging\nweakens over the western Gulf and upper level ridging builds over\nthe southern Plains. Locally, will once again see breezy southerly\nflow develop, with wind gusts near 20 mph. But, drier air\nadvecting into the area will begin to limit coverage of showers\nand storms. In fact, most areas look to remain dry, with just\n20-30% PoPs generally west of Daytona/Orlando. The best chance\nappears to be along a sea breeze collision near Orlando in the\nlate afternoon/evening. Isolated coverage will remain possible\nover the waters. Gusty winds near 50 mph will remain a threat \nwith any storms, as steering flow will continue support quick-\nmoving activity and increasing dry air in the mid levels builds \nDCAPE to near 1000-1100 J/kg. Temperatures will remain slightly\nabove normal, with highs in the lower to mid-90s and heat indices\n102-107 F.\n\nWednesday-Thursday...The Atlantic ridge axis builds westward\ntowards the Florida peninsula midweek, as TD 2 continues westward.\nOverall dry air lingers over the local area, as subsidence builds\naloft. Thus, below average rain chances are forecast Wednesday and\nThursday (generally 20-30% or less). Any showers and storms that\ndevelop are mainly forecast for the afternoons/evenings along the\nsea breezes. Southerly flow weakens with the pressure gradient,\nfavoring a more typical sea breeze pattern driven by the Atlantic\nridge. With less coverage of showers and storms, temperatures\ncreep up a degree or two over the interior, approaching the upper\n90s in some spots. Despite the somewhat drier air, increasing\ntemperatures and very warm overnight lows will allow for building\npockets of Extreme HeatRisk over the interior. Make sure to factor\nheat safety into your plans this week.\n\nFriday-Monday...(previous) Upper level high pressure over the \nPlains and the Deep South will remain in place as a trough over \nthe Midwest shifts eastward towards the Atlantic coast through \nSunday. At the surface, the Atlantic high pressure will shift \nsouthward through the weekend, as a low pressure system pushes \noff the coast of the Carolinas. An associated boundary will dig \nthrough the Deep South into late week and into northern Florida \nbefore stalling over the weekend. Locally, moisture will increase \nover the area from the stalled boundary. However, there remains \nsome uncertainty to how much moisture will push into Florida, and \nwill depend on how far south the boundary makes it and where it \nstalls. For now have a low (20-30 percent) chance for rain and \nstorms on Friday, mainly across the south, and a low to medium \n(20-50 percent) chance area-wide for the weekend. Daily sea\nbreezes at the coast, though prevailing flow looks to veer\nsouthwesterly through the period. Hot temperatures will continue,\nwith afternoon highs in the low to mid 90s, with Saturday being \nthe warmest day of the weekend. Coupled with high humidity, peak \nheat indices will be in the above 100 F. Overnight lows will be in\nthe mid to upper 70s.",
